WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.567
-Welcome, welcome, welcome to
"The Tonight Show."

00:00:03.567 --> 00:00:05.934
You're here. You made it.
Thank you for watching at home.

00:00:05.934 --> 00:00:09.000
You guys, New York City
is buzzing tonight because

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.701
last night in Toronto,
Yankees superstar Aaron Judge

00:00:12.701 --> 00:00:15.467
hit his 61st home run
of the season,

00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:19.234
tying Roger Maris'
American League record.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.467
Yep, Aaron Judge
did the impossible.

00:00:22.467 --> 00:00:25.701
He made non-Yankee fans
root for the Yankees.

00:00:25.701 --> 00:00:26.901
It was a special moment.

00:00:26.901 --> 00:00:28.434
New Yorkers haven't
cheered like this since

00:00:28.434 --> 00:00:29.767
Bill de Blasio left office.

00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:31.467
It was really amazing.

00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:34.100
Fans waited 61 years for
this to happen.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:37.267
That's right -- six decades of
waiting for one exciting moment.

00:00:37.267 --> 00:00:40.467
That sums up baseball
pretty well.

00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:42.334
It was a historic night
for Aaron Judge,

00:00:42.334 --> 00:00:44.467
and you could tell the announcer
was pretty excited,

00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:46.200
but I'm not sure about his
home-run call.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.334
Take a listen to this.

00:00:47.334 --> 00:00:50.300
-Swung on
and drilled deep left field.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:51.968
It is gone!

00:00:51.968 --> 00:00:54.033
He's done it! Move over, 69!

00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:56.901
The new hot thing to do
in the bedroom is 61!

00:00:56.901 --> 00:00:59.400
Its bom to the bom-bom.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.501
He's rounding the bases
like a senior on prom night!

00:01:02.501 --> 00:01:04.634
He's about to hear something
I never heard from my dad,

00:01:04.634 --> 00:01:07.400
which is,
"I'm proud of you, Son."

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.701
Man, oh, man,
that ball left faster

00:01:09.701 --> 00:01:11.701
than my wife with the pool boy.

00:01:11.701 --> 00:01:13.634
Oh, baby!

00:01:13.634 --> 00:01:16.234
-He had weeks to practice that.

00:01:16.234 --> 00:01:18.267
Wasn't even appropriate,
I thought.

00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:19.701
-Very emotional for him.
-Yeah.

00:01:19.701 --> 00:01:22.200
I thought -- Of course, a lot of
people went from the game

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.000
hoping to catch
the home-run ball.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.934
You go the game, "Oh, my gosh.
If I catch this..."

00:01:25.934 --> 00:01:27.834
It was worth a
quarter of a million dollars

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:28.968
if you caught the ball.

00:01:28.968 --> 00:01:30.934
And one guy came pretty close.

00:01:30.934 --> 00:01:32.601
Here he is. Look at this.

00:01:32.601 --> 00:01:36.701
Look at --
Less than 1 foot away.

00:01:36.701 --> 00:01:38.434
Yeah. Yeah. Oh, my gosh.

00:01:38.434 --> 00:01:39.968
And then every guy
who showed up to the game with

00:01:39.968 --> 00:01:42.467
a fishing net was like,
"Well, who's laughing now?"

00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:44.901
And get this -- the guy --
This is real.

00:01:44.901 --> 00:01:47.334
The guy who almost caught
the ball was a Toronto Blue Jays

00:01:47.334 --> 00:01:51.434
fan named -- and this is real --
Frankie Lasagna.

00:01:53.601 --> 00:01:55.000
-What?
-Yeah.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.434
-Frankie Lasagna?

00:01:56.434 --> 00:01:58.868
-Frankie Lasagna
is that fan right there, yeah.

00:01:58.868 --> 00:02:00.868
Frankie Lasagna -- it sounds
like a name you get from

00:02:00.868 --> 00:02:04.234
the Olive Garden
Witness Protection Program.

00:02:04.234 --> 00:02:06.434
Frankie Lasagna sounds like
the name Robert De Niro

00:02:06.434 --> 00:02:08.467
checks into hotels
to avoid paparazzi.

00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:10.601
He's like...
-Hey!

00:02:10.601 --> 00:02:13.067
-Frankie Lasagna sounds like
the name of Trump's next lawyer.

00:02:13.067 --> 00:02:16.834
He's like, "Frankie Lasagna
is the greatest."

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:19.767
Speaking of Trump,
it was just revealed

00:02:19.767 --> 00:02:21.734
that during the
FBI search of Mar-a-Lago,

00:02:21.734 --> 00:02:24.934
they seized 200,000 pages
of documents

00:02:24.934 --> 00:02:26.334
from the former president.

00:02:26.334 --> 00:02:28.167
200,000 pages.

00:02:28.167 --> 00:02:30.033
He's like a grandpa
who prints every e-mail.

00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:32.000
He's like...

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.267
Yeah, if the FBI delayed
their search another day,

00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:37.000
the TV show "Hoarders"
would have beat them to it.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.133
Some more news.

00:02:38.133 --> 00:02:40.467
I read the Nord Stream
pipelines,

00:02:40.467 --> 00:02:42.467
which funneled
gas from Russia to Europe,

00:02:42.467 --> 00:02:45.200
has started leaking
due to underwater explosions.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:48.534
And the U.S. and Europe think
that Russia is responsible.

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:50.534
-Ooh!
-Yeah, a leaky pipeline.

00:02:50.534 --> 00:02:51.701
Today, President Biden was like,

00:02:51.701 --> 00:02:53.934
"Oh, I have a
great urologist for that."

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:57.167
Hey, guys, listen to this.

00:02:57.167 --> 00:02:58.534
A new poll shows that
more Democrats

00:02:58.534 --> 00:03:01.767
are now saying they want Biden
to run for re-election,

00:03:01.767 --> 00:03:03.734
and if he doesn't,
Vice President Harris

00:03:03.734 --> 00:03:04.934
is their top choice.

00:03:04.934 --> 00:03:08.300
That poll was conducted
among two people --

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.467
Joe Biden and Kamala Harris.

00:03:15.534 --> 00:03:17.367
Meanwhile,
I saw that Bernie Sanders

00:03:17.367 --> 00:03:19.567
is leaving the door open
for another presidential run

00:03:19.567 --> 00:03:20.868
in 2024.

00:03:20.868 --> 00:03:23.167
He'll be 83 years old.

00:03:23.167 --> 00:03:24.634
When other members of
the House heard about this,

00:03:24.634 --> 00:03:25.934
they had some thoughts.

00:03:25.934 --> 00:03:28.567
For example, first up,
Congressman Ken Buck said...

00:03:34.634 --> 00:03:37.133
Next up,
Congressman Jim Banks said...

00:03:43.667 --> 00:03:45.667
And Congressman
Tom Cotton said...

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.634
And Congressman Colin Allred
said...

00:03:57.367 --> 00:03:59.801
And Congressman Virginia Foxx
said...

00:04:05.534 --> 00:04:07.767
And Congressman Dusty Johnson
said...

00:04:19.467 --> 00:04:21.501
"My full name."
-His full name.

00:04:21.501 --> 00:04:24.234
♪♪
Oy!

00:04:24.234 --> 00:04:26.267
-And, finally,
Senator Roy Blunt said...

00:04:31.334 --> 00:04:33.367
There you go.
A lot of opinions there.

00:04:33.367 --> 00:04:36.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:36.767 --> 00:04:38.000
Did you guys see this?

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.834
The other night
at her concert in D.C.,

00:04:39.834 --> 00:04:42.334
Lizzo played
President James Madison's

00:04:42.334 --> 00:04:45.267
never-used crystal flute.

00:04:45.267 --> 00:04:47.534
Yeah. Lizzo said, "I just --"
This is real.

00:04:47.534 --> 00:04:49.868
She said, "I just twerked and
played James Madison's

00:04:49.868 --> 00:04:51.367
crystal flute from the 1800s.

00:04:51.367 --> 00:04:52.801
It's pretty cool."

00:04:52.801 --> 00:04:55.968
Well, not many people know this,
but here in the NBC archives,

00:04:55.968 --> 00:04:58.000
we actually have
Calvin Coolidge's saxophone.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:59.968
-Oh!
-This is real. Check this out.

00:04:59.968 --> 00:05:01.467
We brought it out
of the archives. That's cool.

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:04.367
I mean, I could play it,
but I just don't know.

00:05:04.367 --> 00:05:05.567
I mean, I've never --
I don't think I should.

00:05:05.567 --> 00:05:07.033
-Oh, come on.

00:05:07.033 --> 00:05:08.334
-No, I mean,
it's the original...

00:05:08.334 --> 00:05:10.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:12.734 --> 00:05:13.934
Calvin Coolidge.

00:05:13.934 --> 00:05:15.801
This is kind of a --

00:05:15.801 --> 00:05:18.767
I don't know if I
even remember how to do it.

00:05:19.701 --> 00:05:22.667
[ Saxophone plays ]

00:05:22.667 --> 00:05:25.267
-Wow!

00:05:25.267 --> 00:05:27.267
[ "Careless Whisper" playing ]

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:01.467
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:04.634 --> 00:06:08.467
Guys --
-Little story there.

00:06:08.467 --> 00:06:09.834
-What's that?
-Little story there. Wow.

00:06:09.834 --> 00:06:10.901
-Oh, I don't know
what you're talking about.

00:06:10.901 --> 00:06:12.801
-Oh, no. Never mind.

00:06:12.801 --> 00:06:15.234
-Well, guys,
there's a lot of buzz around

00:06:15.234 --> 00:06:16.667
the new horror movie "Smile."

00:06:16.667 --> 00:06:18.133
Did you hear about this movie?

00:06:18.133 --> 00:06:20.167
Everybody -- This is going to be
the big movie this weekend.

00:06:20.167 --> 00:06:21.601
It's expected to be
such a big hit,

00:06:21.601 --> 00:06:24.601
they're already planning on
making a franchise out of it.

00:06:24.601 --> 00:06:26.501
Yeah, take a look at
the trailer I just saw.

00:06:26.501 --> 00:06:30.067
-This Friday, don't miss
the premiere of "Smile,"

00:06:30.067 --> 00:06:32.834
and then be sure to catch all
the heart-stopping sequels...

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:51.901
-Wow. Very interesting.

00:06:51.901 --> 00:06:53.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:57.067 --> 00:06:58.234
Well, some business news.

00:06:58.234 --> 00:07:00.167
I saw that Hefty
just released new

00:07:00.167 --> 00:07:02.934
pumpkin-spice-scented
trash bags.

00:07:02.934 --> 00:07:04.801
Right now,
all the raccoons are like,

00:07:04.801 --> 00:07:06.901
"I know it's basic,
but I still love it, okay?

00:07:06.901 --> 00:07:08.367
Can I just say?"

00:07:08.367 --> 00:07:09.400
Well, this is interesting.

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:11.100
According to new research,

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:14.901
the pandemic may have changed
people's personalities forever.

00:07:14.901 --> 00:07:16.968
I'm not sure I've noticed anyone
acting differently.

00:07:16.968 --> 00:07:18.534
I mean, what about you, Higgins?

00:07:18.534 --> 00:07:21.868
-Wouldn't know about that, Jack.
[ Jazz music playing ]

00:07:21.868 --> 00:07:24.434
I've always been the same.
Take it from Baccarat Steve.

00:07:24.434 --> 00:07:26.234
All Baccarat Steve
has ever cared about is

00:07:26.234 --> 00:07:28.567
the click, click, clickedy-clack
of the roulette wheel,

00:07:28.567 --> 00:07:29.701
seeing those triple cherries

00:07:29.701 --> 00:07:31.300
come up on
my favorite slot machine,

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:34.634
and shoplifting crab legs from
Circus Circus buffet.

00:07:34.634 --> 00:07:38.234
Mwah! Come on! Lucky 4s.

00:07:43.234 --> 00:07:45.934
-Quest, have you noticed Higgins
has been acting differently?

00:07:45.934 --> 00:07:47.434
-[ Chuckles ]
[ Rock music playing ]

00:07:47.434 --> 00:07:50.200
I don't pay mind
to anybody but me,

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:52.234
my leather jacket, and my Hog.

00:07:52.234 --> 00:07:53.267
[ Chuckles ]

00:07:53.267 --> 00:07:55.901
She's a '78 Harley Shovelhead,

00:07:55.901 --> 00:07:58.334
and there's nothing more
than I love in life

00:07:58.334 --> 00:08:01.033
than a little whipping her down
the South Dakota prairie,

00:08:01.033 --> 00:08:03.667
hitting 80 miles per hour
while the old lady

00:08:03.667 --> 00:08:08.033
sits behind and feeds me
meatballs from Olive Garden.

00:08:13.367 --> 00:08:15.033
-James, do you think
people's personalities

00:08:15.033 --> 00:08:16.901
changed because of the pandemic?

00:08:16.901 --> 00:08:19.167
-You know me, Jimmy.

00:08:19.167 --> 00:08:22.934
I've always been the same,
a fan of all things

00:08:22.934 --> 00:08:25.701
science fic--
[ Laughter ]

00:08:25.701 --> 00:08:29.067
Science fiction
and geek culture.

00:08:29.067 --> 00:08:31.767
I'm the biggest fan
of "House of the Dragon"

00:08:31.767 --> 00:08:36.167
this side of Westeros, LOL.

00:08:37.634 --> 00:08:39.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:45.634 --> 00:08:47.534
I've even learned
the language spoken

00:08:47.534 --> 00:08:50.501
by the elves on the show,
High Valyrian.

00:08:50.501 --> 00:08:52.367
Check this out.
[ Clears throat ]

00:10:00.501 --> 00:10:02.501
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:09.968
-What?
-I had no idea.

00:10:09.968 --> 00:10:13.734
-Okay. Thank you.
-Yeah.

00:10:13.734 --> 00:10:15.667
-Thank you, James.
-Wow, I guess.

00:10:15.667 --> 00:10:17.200
-I don't know.
-Wow, man.

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:19.167
-That's odd.
-Wow.

00:10:21.234 --> 00:10:24.234
Finally...
[ Laughter ]

00:10:24.234 --> 00:10:26.133
...a new survey
found that over a quarter --

00:10:26.133 --> 00:10:29.601
I should let everyone at home
know that James

00:10:29.601 --> 00:10:32.634
never saw those cue cards or
wore that wig until just now.

00:10:32.634 --> 00:10:33.734
He had no idea.

00:10:33.734 --> 00:10:36.667
We just surprised him
on the spot.

00:10:36.667 --> 00:10:38.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:44.934 --> 00:10:48.200
Finally, a new survey found
that over a quarter of Americans

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:50.367
have been so hangry
that they've actually

00:10:50.367 --> 00:10:51.968
eaten out of the garbage.

00:10:51.968 --> 00:10:54.501
The most common explanation
they gave,

00:10:54.501 --> 00:10:57.133
"For some reason, the trash bags
smell like pumpkin spice."

